# Example CLIWM config. This file is written to ~/.config/cliwm.conf
# automatically the first time you run CLIWM if that path doesn't exist yet.
#
# Format (Hyprland-flavored):
#   leader      = <mods>, <key>          chord that starts a "leader, digit" sequence
#   resize_exit = <mods>, <key>          the only chord that exits resize mode (default: esc)
#   accent      = #<hex>                 overrides the focused-pane border color
#   statusbar   = <shell command>        see "Status bar" below
#   layout      = one-side|grid|divide   see "Layouts" below (default: one-side)
#   bind        = <mods>, <key>, <action>[, <arg>]
#
# mods: none, or ctrl/alt/shift combined with '+' (e.g. ctrl+shift)
# key: a single character, up/down/left/right, enter, tab, esc, space,
#      backspace, or f1-f12
# actions: new_terminal | move_window (arg: up/down/left/right)
#        | kill_window | quit | toggle_resize_mode
#        | switch_workspace (arg: 1-9)
#
# `leader` must carry a modifier -- a bare, unmodified key would swallow
# every normal keystroke typed in a shell that happens to match it. The
# default uses Alt rather than Ctrl for the digit half specifically because
# most terminals don't send a distinguishable Ctrl+<digit> for every digit
# (Ctrl+1/2/3/8/9 collide with other keys or arrive with no modifier bit on
# many terminals) -- Alt+<digit> works reliably for all of 1-9.
#
# Two behaviors are built in and not remappable here:
#   - Alt+<digit 1-9>                   focuses window N in the current workspace
#   - leader, then digit 1-9 held with the SAME modifier as leader, switches
#     to workspace N (default: hold Alt, press w, then -- still holding Alt
#     -- press a digit)
#
# To bind a *specific* workspace to its own single chord instead of (or in
# addition to) the leader sequence above, use switch_workspace directly:
#   bind = alt, f1, switch_workspace, 1
#   bind = alt, f2, switch_workspace, 2
#
# A workspace holds at most 9 windows, matching the 1-9 digit range used to
# focus them -- new_terminal is a no-op past that.
#
# Layouts:
#   one-side  each new window splits whichever pane is currently focused,
#             always into rows (top/bottom) -- so opening several terminals
#             without changing focus builds a top-to-bottom stack, same as
#             focusing a specific one and splitting just that pane.
#   grid      all windows recomputed fresh into a roughly-square grid every
#             time, independent of focus/creation history.
#   divide    like one-side, but each split's direction (rows or columns)
#             is chosen from the aspect ratio of the pane being split, so
#             the result tends to look grid-like without being a flat grid.
# Only one-side and divide support resize mode's up/down (grid's cells are
# always uniform); all three support left/right and moving windows.

leader = alt, w
# resize_exit = esc

# accent = #61afef

# layout = one-side

# Status bar: CLIWM doesn't ship one, but this is the hook to build one.
# Point it at a long-lived program -- not a pane, not a terminal, launched
# once and left running independently of any workspace for as long as
# CLIWM is open (and killed when CLIWM exits) -- and CLIWM shows whatever
# line it prints in the bottom-left corner (the version label stays
# bottom-right), updating whenever a new line arrives. The program decides
# its own update cadence (an i3status/waybar-style loop printing a fresh
# line every second, or only when something actually changes); CLIWM never
# re-invokes or polls it.
# statusbar = ~/.config/cliwm-statusbar.sh

bind = alt, t, new_terminal
bind = alt, up, move_window, up
bind = alt, down, move_window, down
bind = alt, left, move_window, left
bind = alt, right, move_window, right
bind = alt, q, quit
bind = alt, x, kill_window

# Resize mode: press once to enter, then arrow keys grow/shrink the
# focused window (left/right adjust the main/stack split, up/down adjust
# its height against a stack neighbor); Esc (or the same chord again) exits.
bind = alt, r, toggle_resize_mode